			<div class="content" id="post-content-2239353">
									

<p>That's not a really great example because metastable is wider than that. *puts engineer hat on*</p>
<p>For a practical example of a metastable state, take a PVC drinks bottle. When you put hot water in it, it shrinks and becomes opaque, right? That's because the transparent and comparably lower density polyethylene is a metastable state of denser, opaque, crystalline PE, and if you give the molecules a bit of a thermal kick so that they can actually get to move around, the polymer chains will rearrange themselves into the actually stable state.</p>
<p>In fact, a lot of these states (another example would be martensite produced by quenching steel) follow the same general rule of being an unstable state preserved by kinetics.</p>
<p>It's kinda like&#8230; say we have stable states. You at work, you at home and you at Costco, say, with you not moving much (aka, order of 10m displacement from initial point inside the building) while there because you got things to do/like being there.<br />
Now, you end up in your car to go from work to home ,and suddenly, there's a traffic jam. Now, a silly outside observer could say that you're in a stable state because you aren't moving - the fact is that you're trying to go home, but can't, because everyone else who is also trying to go home and can't is blocking your path, originally caused by a snow drift. And, unless someone removes the snow drift (adds energy, in this case mechanical by plowing it away) &#8230; yeah, no, you aren't going anywhere.</p>
<p>Congratulation, the clogged highway is now a perfect analogy of a metastable state.</p>

